<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<!--
  Cleartext policy for the app.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
  Razorpay's checkout runs a device-fingerprinting step before it will validate
  an account for netbanking. Part of that step probes a handful of loopback
  ports over plain HTTP (http://localhost:7070/<random>.png and friends) to
  detect emulators and remote-control tooling.

  In a browser those probes succeed: Chrome treats http://localhost as a
  "potentially trustworthy" origin, so it is exempt from the mixed-content rule
  that would otherwise block plain HTTP loaded by an HTTPS page. Android's
  WebView has no such exemption — since API 28 the platform default is
  cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" for everything, so each probe died with
  ERR_CLEARTEXT_NOT_PERMITTED, the fingerprint never completed, and
  POST /v1/standard_checkout/payments/validate/account answered 500.

  That is the entire difference between "netbanking works on the website" and
  "netbanking fails in the app" — same key, same backend, same account. Card
  payments were unaffected because they do not go through account validation.

  WHY THIS IS SAFE
  Cleartext is permitted ONLY for loopback. Traffic to 127.0.0.1 / ::1 never
  leaves the handset, so there is no network path for anyone to read or tamper
  with it — the usual objection to cleartext does not apply. Everything that
  does leave the device stays under base-config, which keeps cleartext off, so
  API calls, JWTs and payment traffic are still HTTPS-only and would fail
  loudly rather than silently downgrade.

  Deliberately NOT done: flipping Capacitor's allowMixedContent to true. That
  is a blanket permission for any plain-HTTP subresource on any page the
  WebView loads, including third-party checkout pages. This grants the one
  thing that is actually needed and nothing else.
-->
<network-security-config>

    <!-- Everything not named below: HTTPS only. -->
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" />

    <!-- Loopback only. Never traverses a network. -->
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">localhost</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
    </domain-config>

</network-security-config>
